## What this changes

**Typing in place.** A text box was written and changed in the composer card beside it: the Text box tool's click opened "Add a comment" and a double-click on a box opened "Edit comment", away from the text. Now, as in Acrobat:

- the Text box tool's click opens an editor **where the box goes**; a double-click (or *Edit text…* in the context menu) on a text box or typewriter text edits it **in place**;
- the editor shows the box's own colour, font size and fill at the page's zoom, grows with the text as the box will, and covers the box's old drawing while it is open (blue frame);
- clicking away or ⌘/Ctrl+Enter keeps the text (Enter is a new line), Escape drops the change; a click while a box is being typed into only ends that, the next one starts another box;
- Save keeps what is being typed (`commit_typing_in`), and an open editor with changes counts as unsaved work (close prompts);
- callouts (whose box sits inside their rectangle, `/RD`) and text on a turned page or view keep the composer card.

**A box that fits its text keeps fitting it.** The box followed new text in height only: shorter text left it as wide as before, longer text wrapped at the old width, and a smaller font size never shrank it, so it had to be stretched or squeezed by hand. Now a box that fits its text — as wide as a new box for that text would be (`appearance::text_box_width`, the one width rule for new and refitted boxes; a box made before at the old 40 pt minimum counts too) — keeps fitting it when the text (`set_contents`) or the font size (`set_font_size`) changes, narrower and shorter as well as wider. A box **sized by hand** keeps its width (and, for a smaller font, its height) as before. A short text's box is no longer at least 40 pt wide but one em.

